WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
XGBoost.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:07.700
The library that ruled tabular competitions for a decade, built on one clever idea.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.400
A forest builds its trees in parallel and averages them.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.367
Gradient boosting builds them one after another.

5
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:19.767
Each new tree is trained on the errors the ensemble has left so far.

6
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:24.700
Dozens to thousands of small corrections compound into a very strong model.

7
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.900
Here is the loop.

8
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.300
Predict with the trees you have so far.

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.300
Measure the residuals, meaning what is still wrong.

10
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.467
Fit a small new tree to those errors.

11
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.555
And add it in, scaled by a small learning rate.

12
00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:38.267
Then repeat.

13
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:41.967
The knob that matters most is early stopping.

14
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:44.533
Training loss keeps falling with every round.

15
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:47.724
But validation loss bottoms out and then rises.

16
00:00:47.724 --> 00:00:51.967
Stop there, and the model picks its own size instead of you guessing.

17
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:54.367
Here it is in code.

18
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.633
We allow up to two thousand trees, only as a cap.

19
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:00.200
Each step is small, with shallow trees.

20
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.267
Stop after fifty rounds with no improvement.

21
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:06.133
And we hand it a validation set to watch.

22
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.967
Now the result on our exoplanet features.

23
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:13.343
The forest plateaus at zero point nine AUC.

24
00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:20.282
XGBoost with early stopping reaches zero point nine three, stopping at round four hundred twelve.

25
00:01:20.282 --> 00:01:25.933
Those three points mean fewer wasted follow up observations per real candidate.

26
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:27.879
Let's recap.

27
00:01:27.879 --> 00:01:30.367
Boosting builds trees one after another.

28
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.800
Each tree fixes the residual errors.

29
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.000
XGBoost adds regularization and speed.

30
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.311
And early stopping picks the model size.

31
00:01:39.311 --> 00:01:40.667
Next up: LightGBM.
